      "text": "To find the largest divisor of 342 that is also a factor of 285, we first need to determine the greatest common divisor (GCD) of the two numbers. The GCD is the largest positive integer that divides both 342 and 285 without leaving a remainder.\n\nLet's start by finding the prime factorizations of 342 and 285.\n\nFor 285:\n\\[ 285 \\div 3 = 95 \\]\n\\[ 95 \\div 5 = 19 \\]\n\\[ 19 \\div 19 = 1 \\]\nSo, the prime factorization of 285 is:\n\\[ 285 = 3 \\times 5 \\times 19 \\]\n\nFor 342:\n\\[ 342 \\div 2 = 171 \\]\n\\[ 171 \\div 3 = 57 \\]\n\\[ 57 \\div 3 = 19 \\]\n\\[ 19 \\div 19 = 1 \\]\nSo, the prime factorization of 342 is:\n\\[ 342 = 2 \\times 3^2 \\times 19 \\]\n\nNext, we identify the common factors of 285 and 342. The prime factorizations show that the common factors are \\(3\\) and \\(19\\).\n\nTo find the GCD, we take the lowest power of each common prime factor:\nThe lowest power of \\(3\\) in both factorizations is \\(3^1\\), and the lowest power of \\(19\\) in both factorizations is \\(19^1\\).\n\nTherefore, the GCD of 342 and 285 is:\n\\[ \\text{GCD} = 3 \\times 19 = 57 \\]\n\nThus, the largest divisor of 342 that is also a factor of 285 is \\(\\boxed{57}\\).",
