      "text": "To find the largest divisor of 342 that is also a factor of 285, we need to determine the greatest common divisor (GCD) of the two numbers. The GCD is the largest positive integer that divides both numbers without leaving a remainder.\n\nWe will use the Euclidean algorithm to find the GCD of 342 and 285. The Euclidean algorithm is based on the principle that the GCD of two numbers also divides their difference.\n\nHere are the steps:\n\n1. Divide 342 by 285 and find the remainder.\n   \\[\n   342 \\div 285 = 1 \\quad \\text{remainder} \\quad 342 - 285 \\times 1 = 57\n   \\]\n   So, we have:\n   \\[\n   342 = 285 \\times 1 + 57\n   \\]\n\n2. Now, replace 342 with 285 and 285 with 57, and repeat the process.\n   \\[\n   285 \\div 57 = 5 \\quad \\text{remainder} \\quad 285 - 57 \\times 5 = 18\n   \\]\n   So, we have:\n   \\[\n   285 = 57 \\times 5 + 18\n   \\]\n\n3. Next, replace 285 with 57 and 57 with 18, and repeat the process.\n   \\[\n   57 \\div 18 = 3 \\quad \\text{remainder} \\quad 57 - 18 \\times 3 = 9\n   \\]\n   So, we have:\n   \\[\n   57 = 18 \\times 3 + 9\n   \\]\n\n4. Replace 57 with 18 and 18 with 9, and repeat the process.\n   \\[\n   18 \\div 9 = 2 \\quad \\text{remainder} \\quad 18 - 9 \\times 2 = 0\n   \\]\n   So, we have:\n   \\[\n   18 = 9 \\times 2 + 0\n   \\]\n\nSince the remainder is now 0, the GCD is the last non-zero remainder, which is 9. Therefore, the largest divisor of 342 that is also a factor of 285 is \\(\\boxed{9}\\).",
